# Controller Attached Flash Sharing: GET_FLASH_NP and PUT_FLASH_C.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. The capture has no flash traffic -- it
# never even enables the channel -- so nothing here has independent evidence
# behind it.
#
# THE TWO OPCODES ARE A PAIR AND THE PAIRING IS THE POINT. Section 4.2.4.1,
# p.78: "The Controller Attached Flash Sharing scheme uses 2 dedicated eSPI
# command opcodes: GET_FLASH_NP and PUT_FLASH_C." The flash sits on the
# controller, the target asks for access, and the controller answers in a
# *separate transaction* later.
#
# That is why neither response phase below carries a completion, and it is the
# one place these packets differ from their peripheral equivalents. PUT_NP does
# get a completion back in its own response phase -- Figure 24, p.38, captioned
# "Connected Controller Initiated Non-Posted Transaction". Copying that here is
# the obvious mistake, and the giveaway that it is wrong is that the flash
# channel would not need PUT_FLASH_C or the FLASH_C_FREE status bit if it were
# true.
#
# --- 1. GET_FLASH_NP: the target issues a Flash Read request ---
#
# "Used in Controller Attached Flash Sharing mode for the target to issue a
# flash access request to the controller" (Table 2, p.26). So the *response*
# phase carries the request, the same way GET_NP's does in Figure 27, p.41.
#
#   09     GET_FLASH_NP                        Table 2, p.26
#   3F     CRC
#
#   08     ACCEPT
#   00     Cycle Type: Flash Read              Table 5, p.48
#
#          00h is ALSO Memory Read 32 on the peripheral channel. Table 5 note 3,
#          p.49, allows that outright, and Table 16 on p.81 lists 00h as Read
#          again, so the encoding is confirmed twice on two pages. A lookup
#          keyed by the byte alone reports a memory read here.
#
#   20     Tag 2, Length[11:8] = 0h
#   40     Length[7:0] = 40h -> 64 bytes requested
#
#          A read request has a Length and no payload: section 4.1.3, p.50,
#          "For memory read and Flash Read, the length field specifies the data
#          payload size requested." Table 16's Length column for the 00h row is
#          "<= Max Read Request Size", which is register 040h bits 14:12.
#
#   00 FF 00 00   Address, most significant byte first    Figure 48, p.75
#
#          Seven header bytes, not three: Figure 48 gives Flash Read the same
#          7-byte header as Flash Write, address included.
#
#   0F 01  Status
#   9F     CRC
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).
CMD 09 3F
TAR
RSP 08 00 20 40 00 FF 00 00 0F 01 9F

# --- 2. PUT_FLASH_C: the controller returns the completion ---
#
# "Used in Controller Attached Flash Sharing mode for the controller to return
# a flash access completion to the target" (Table 2, p.26). The command phase
# carries the completion and the response is only status, which is the shape
# Figure 27 p.41 draws for the peripheral PUT_PC completion.
#
#   08     PUT_FLASH_C                         Table 2, p.26
#   0F     Cycle Type: 00001 P1P0 1 with P1P0 = 11b
#          Successful Completion With Data, "only completion for a split
#          completion sequence" -- Table 5 p.48 and note 1 p.49. The raised 1
#          between P0 and the last bit on the page is a footnote marker.
#   20     Tag 2, Length[11:8] = 0h            matches the request's tag
#
#          "The Tag field is used to match the completion with the request.
#          Flash access requests with the same tag must be completed in order"
#          (section 4.2.4, p.75).
#
#   04     Length[7:0] = 04h -> 4 bytes
#   AA BB CC DD   Data                         Figure 49, p.75
#
#          Three header bytes here, not seven. A completion has no address --
#          Figure 49 draws it exactly as Figure 39 draws the peripheral one,
#          which is what section 4.2.4 p.75 means by "The Flash Access channel
#          uses the same packet format as the eSPI Peripheral Channel
#          transactions".
#
#   BC     CRC
CMD 08 0F 20 04 AA BB CC DD BC
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95
